  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 221 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\n(Thursday Night - Sunday)\n\nIncreasing chances of rain and a few storms are forecast beginning \nlate Thursday night as a midlevel shortwave trough moves into the \nmid-Missouri Valley/Upper Midwest. This feature has trended a bit \nmore to the north over the past 36-48 hours, and has consequently \nshifted the best threat for widespread (and potentially \nmore significant) rainfall totals northward as well. It still \nlooks like our area will likely see at least some rainfall \nthough with a slow-moving cold frontal passage, but the higher \ntotals are likely to stay to the north where the stronger \nmid/upper level forcing is expected to reside. Probabilities for\nat least 1\" of rain through Sunday on the LREF are in the \n20-40% range, and even the 95th percentile is at or below 2.5\".\nTherefore, it does not appear that heavy rainfall/flash \nflooding is likely at this time. There also does not appear to \nbe any severe threat with limited instability most likely. GEFS \nchances for 1000+ J/kg of SBCAPE stay below 50% on Friday \nafternoon/evening, and drop further this weekend.\n\nTemperature wise, below normal daytime readings with values closer \nto normal overnight are expected with plenty of cloud cover through \nthe period along with rain chances. Spread in the NBM IQR is pretty \nlow even out through Sunday, generally at or below 5 degrees. Even \nthe 75th percentile of the NBM shows below normal high temperatures, \nfurther increasing confidence in the forecast below normal highs.\n\n(Sunday Night - Next Tuesday)\n\nForecast uncertainty increases heading into early next week with \nrespect to how the midlevel shortwave trough evolves. More members \nfrom the grand ensemble show it cutting off (~80%) looking at the \n500-hPa height clusters, but exactly where is uncertain. A majority \nof members from the cutoff cluster show it near the lower Ohio \nValley, though a smaller percentage are farther to the west. \nAssuming the low is to our southeast, continued cool \ntemperatures would be expected along with dry weather. However, \nif the low is cutoff farther west, increased rain chances would \nexist. Either scenario though would continue the below normal \ntemperatures, particularly during the day.",
